\begin{abstract}
Multi-agent pipelines can be expensive and are not always better. A plan--execute--review team
costs up to six times a single reasoning agent, and on some tasks it misses an answer a single agent gets right,
mostly because its first draft is already wrong and its reviewer does not correct it. Skipping it when it is not
needed is the obvious saving, but rules for when to skip can fail silently: a confidence threshold
of similar cost lost up to 16 points of accuracy. We ask instead how much accuracy a user is
willing to give up, and spend as little as possible within that. \climb{} climbs from a direct answer to
a single agent to the team, stopping on confidence and agreement, and deploys the cheapest policy it can certify with a distribution-free guarantee. Because skipping the team can
also keep correct answers the team would miss, the loss is signed, and testing it with a variance-adaptive betting test typically
certifies more than clipping it or applying a Hoeffding--Bentkus bound. When no cheaper
policy can be certified, \climb{} runs the team, so a misleading signal tends to cost savings rather than
accuracy. Across three open models and four benchmarks, \climb{} exceeded its tolerance in at most 3\% of
splits against a nominal 10\%, while saving 38\% of tokens on average at a 5-point tolerance and 65\% at
10 points.
\end{abstract}
